1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:03,456
A water plant in a glass, next to a lamp.

2
00:00:03,456 --> 00:00:06,391
Tiny bubbles of oxygen
rise from its leaves.

3
00:00:06,391 --> 00:00:09,477
Bring the lamp closer,
and more bubbles come.

4
00:00:09,877 --> 00:00:12,629
Closer still, and the
bubbles stop increasing.

5
00:00:12,629 --> 00:00:14,610
More light, but no more speed.

6
00:00:15,060 --> 00:00:16,517
Diya tried this at home.

7
00:00:16,517 --> 00:00:19,216
She asked: why does the
plant stop speeding up?

8
00:00:19,216 --> 00:00:21,106
And what is holding it back now?

9
00:00:21,456 --> 00:00:23,151
Here is the short answer.

10
00:00:23,151 --> 00:00:25,993
Photosynthesis depends
on many factors at once.

11
00:00:25,993 --> 00:00:28,657
The one in shortest supply sets the speed.

12
00:00:28,657 --> 00:00:30,785
It is called the limiting factor.

13
00:00:31,285 --> 00:00:35,278
By the end, you will know the internal
and external factors, Blackman's

14
00:00:35,278 --> 00:00:39,719
law, and how light, carbon dioxide,
temperature and water change the rate.

15
00:00:40,369 --> 00:00:43,049
First, what affects how
fast a plant makes food?

16
00:00:43,399 --> 00:00:45,873
Some factors are inside the plant.

17
00:00:45,873 --> 00:00:47,844
These are internal factors.

18
00:00:48,294 --> 00:00:50,030
Others come from outside.

19
00:00:50,030 --> 00:00:53,233
Sunlight, temperature,
carbon dioxide and water.

20
00:00:53,233 --> 00:00:55,069
These are external factors.

21
00:00:55,719 --> 00:00:58,698
Let's zoom in and find
the internal factors.

22
00:00:59,048 --> 00:01:00,770
Start with the whole plant.

23
00:01:00,770 --> 00:01:04,360
The number, size, age and
angle of its leaves all matter.

24
00:01:04,860 --> 00:01:06,217
Zoom into one leaf.

25
00:01:06,217 --> 00:01:10,384
The number of mesophyll cells and
chloroplasts matters, and so does the

26
00:01:10,384 --> 00:01:12,364
carbon dioxide inside the leaf.

27
00:01:12,864 --> 00:01:14,499
Zoom into one chloroplast.

28
00:01:14,499 --> 00:01:16,792
The amount of chlorophyll matters too.

29
00:01:16,792 --> 00:01:20,474
All these come from the genes
of the plant and how it grows.

30
00:01:21,124 --> 00:01:24,868
Now watch what happens as
light goes up, step by step.

31
00:01:25,218 --> 00:01:26,118
Step one.

32
00:01:26,032 --> 00:01:29,099
In dim light, the rate
rises as light rises.

33
00:01:29,099 --> 00:01:31,540
Here, light is the limiting factor.

34
00:01:32,290 --> 00:01:33,190
Step two.

35
00:01:33,059 --> 00:01:36,713
Light saturation comes at about
ten percent of full sunlight.

36
00:01:36,713 --> 00:01:41,585
So in nature, light rarely limits a
plant, except in shade or a dense forest.

37
00:01:42,335 --> 00:01:43,246
Step three.

38
00:01:43,246 --> 00:01:46,748
After saturation, more light
does not raise the rate.

39
00:01:46,748 --> 00:01:50,042
Something else now limits
it, like carbon dioxide.

40
00:01:50,792 --> 00:01:51,692
Step four.

41
00:01:51,609 --> 00:01:55,806
Give more carbon dioxide, and the
curve climbs higher before it flattens

42
00:01:55,806 --> 00:01:56,706
again.

43
00:01:57,129 --> 00:01:58,029
Step five.

44
00:01:57,906 --> 00:02:00,382
Too much light can break down chlorophyll.

45
00:02:00,382 --> 00:02:01,713
Then the rate falls.

46
00:02:02,463 --> 00:02:03,471
Remember this.

47
00:02:03,471 --> 00:02:06,277
The factor in shortest
supply sets the speed.

48
00:02:06,877 --> 00:02:08,448
This idea has a name.

49
00:02:08,448 --> 00:02:12,121
Blackman's law of limiting
factors, from nineteen oh five.

50
00:02:12,471 --> 00:02:17,099
If a process is affected by many
factors, its rate is set by the factor

51
00:02:17,099 --> 00:02:19,111
nearest to its minimum value.

52
00:02:19,111 --> 00:02:21,948
Change that factor, and the rate changes.

53
00:02:22,398 --> 00:02:26,234
Picture a green leaf with good
light and good carbon dioxide.

54
00:02:26,234 --> 00:02:29,596
On a very cold day, it
may not make food at all.

55
00:02:29,596 --> 00:02:31,543
Warm it up, and it starts.

56
00:02:32,193 --> 00:02:33,591
Now carbon dioxide.

57
00:02:33,591 --> 00:02:35,828
It is the major limiting factor.

58
00:02:36,178 --> 00:02:40,920
Air has only about zero point zero
three to zero point zero four percent

59
00:02:40,920 --> 00:02:42,102
carbon dioxide.

60
00:02:42,102 --> 00:02:46,558
Raising it to about zero point
zero five percent raises the rate.

61
00:02:46,558 --> 00:02:49,980
More than that, for
long, can harm the plant.

62
00:02:50,430 --> 00:02:54,710
C4 plants are saturated at about three
hundred and sixty microlitres per

63
00:02:54,710 --> 00:02:55,610
litre.

64
00:02:55,308 --> 00:02:58,824
C3 plants keep responding
beyond four hundred and fifty.

65
00:02:58,824 --> 00:03:03,094
So greenhouse growers give tomatoes
and bell peppers extra carbon dioxide

66
00:03:03,094 --> 00:03:04,397
for higher yields.

67
00:03:05,047 --> 00:03:06,154
Two more factors.

68
00:03:06,154 --> 00:03:07,476
Temperature and water.

69
00:03:07,826 --> 00:03:11,251
The dark reaction runs on enzymes,
so temperature controls it.

70
00:03:11,251 --> 00:03:13,552
The light reaction is affected much less.

71
00:03:13,552 --> 00:03:16,723
C4 plants work best at higher
temperatures than C3 plants.

72
00:03:17,173 --> 00:03:19,074
Water acts through the plant.

73
00:03:19,074 --> 00:03:23,368
When water is short, stomata close,
so less carbon dioxide gets in.

74
00:03:23,368 --> 00:03:26,190
Leaves wilt, and their
surface area shrinks.

75
00:03:26,790 --> 00:03:27,690
Your turn.

76
00:03:27,605 --> 00:03:32,271
A plant has bright light, warmth and
water, but very little carbon dioxide.

77
00:03:32,271 --> 00:03:34,153
Will more light speed it up?

78
00:03:34,153 --> 00:03:35,352
Pause and decide.

79
00:03:38,552 --> 00:03:39,452
No!

80
00:03:39,036 --> 00:03:41,676
Carbon dioxide is the limiting factor.

81
00:03:41,676 --> 00:03:44,811
Only more carbon dioxide
will raise the rate.

82
00:03:45,461 --> 00:03:47,037
Now, a worked example.

83
00:03:47,037 --> 00:03:49,573
Find the limiting factor in three plants.

84
00:03:49,923 --> 00:03:53,941
Plant one grows on the floor of
a dense forest, warm and wet.

85
00:03:53,941 --> 00:03:55,864
Its limiting factor is light.

86
00:03:56,314 --> 00:03:59,815
Plant two is a tomato in
a bright, warm greenhouse.

87
00:03:59,815 --> 00:04:02,325
Its limiting factor is carbon dioxide.

88
00:04:02,325 --> 00:04:04,286
That is why growers add it.

89
00:04:04,736 --> 00:04:07,899
Plant three sits in bright
sun on a frosty morning.

90
00:04:07,899 --> 00:04:10,038
Its limiting factor is temperature.

91
00:04:10,038 --> 00:04:11,712
Its enzymes are too cold.

92
00:04:12,362 --> 00:04:13,826
Now, the exam trap.

93
00:04:14,176 --> 00:04:17,634
Many students think more light
always means more photosynthesis.

94
00:04:18,034 --> 00:04:21,054
But after saturation, another
factor limits the rate.

95
00:04:21,054 --> 00:04:23,502
And too much light
breaks down chlorophyll.

96
00:04:24,152 --> 00:04:25,518
Where is this asked?

97
00:04:25,518 --> 00:04:29,514
As statement questions on Blackman's
law, light saturation, and how C3

98
00:04:29,514 --> 00:04:31,962
and C4 plants respond to carbon dioxide.

99
00:04:32,562 --> 00:04:33,711
Exam level now.

100
00:04:33,711 --> 00:04:35,405
Read the four statements.

101
00:04:35,405 --> 00:04:36,603
Pause and solve.

102
00:04:42,003 --> 00:04:43,229
The answer is D.

103
00:04:43,229 --> 00:04:46,002
Statements one, three
and four are correct.

104
00:04:46,502 --> 00:04:49,804
Light saturates at about ten
percent of full sunlight.

105
00:04:49,804 --> 00:04:51,592
Water stress closes stomata.

106
00:04:51,592 --> 00:04:56,200
And the dark reaction is temperature
controlled, because it runs on enzymes.

107
00:04:56,700 --> 00:04:58,284
Statement two is wrong.

108
00:04:58,284 --> 00:05:02,110
Saturation at about three hundred
and sixty is for C4 plants.

109
00:05:02,110 --> 00:05:04,893
C3 plants go beyond
four hundred and fifty.

110
00:05:05,493 --> 00:05:07,033
Here is your recap.

111
00:05:07,033 --> 00:05:08,615
Screenshot this card.

112
00:05:11,815 --> 00:05:14,001
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

113
00:05:14,401 --> 00:05:15,301
One.

114
00:05:14,836 --> 00:05:16,412
What is a limiting factor?

115
00:05:17,312 --> 00:05:18,212
Two.

116
00:05:17,751 --> 00:05:20,485
At about what light level
does saturation happen?

117
00:05:21,385 --> 00:05:22,285
Three.

118
00:05:21,942 --> 00:05:24,610
Why do greenhouse growers
add carbon dioxide?

119
00:05:25,710 --> 00:05:28,475
Next time: plants make food in the light.

120
00:05:28,475 --> 00:05:31,558
But how do they get the
energy back out of it?

121
00:05:31,558 --> 00:05:34,765
We find out in do plants
breathe, and glycolysis.
